    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d101">

                <pb facs="0359" n="989" sameAs="frus:frus1873p1v2/pg989"/>

                <head>No. 407.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">General <persName corresp="#p_DES1"
                            type="from">Sickles</persName> to Mr. <persName corresp="#p_HF1"
                            type="to">Fish</persName>.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">United
                                States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1873-06-01"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 1, 1873</date>. (Received June
                        20.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 610.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to forward herewith a copy
                    of the papers prepared in obedience to your instruction No. 309, in relation to
                    the grievances imposed on foreign shipping by the customs regulations in Cuba.
                    On pages<anchor n="*" xml:id="d101-f1"/> 46–52 of the printed case will be found
                    a draft of a proposed note to the Spanish government. These papers have been
                    transmitted, in duplicate, to the representatives of Great Britain, Germany, and
                    Sweden residing at this capital, accompanied by a note—<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >mutatis mutandis</hi>—corresponding to the copy annexed.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><persName corresp="#p_DES1">D. E. SICKLES</persName>.</signed>
                </closer>









